      "request": "Change heading shape 48 paragraph 1 to Sampling and ablations. Replace shape 49 paragraph 1 with a short explanation that the bar chart compares sampling strategies and the table below tests component removal. Keep all other content and all geometry unchanged. Use the source page 6 Experiments paragraph as evidence. Do not add numbers. Do not protect any textboxes unless explicitly asked."
    },
    {
      "splits": [
        {
          "node": "internal_11a5176479b049a59a3ed208c66e6c86",
          "before": 0.3063462386759568,
          "after": 0.35
        },
        {
          "node": "internal_144982ebd1cd4bf7b97a15fc1d1b5f69",
          "before": 0.6128731828761504,
          "after": 0.54
        }
      ],
      "texts": [
        {
          "shape": "33",
          "paragraph": 1,
          "text": "• IO2Code infers hidden rules from I/O alone.",
          "page": 3,
          "quote": "It has no access to the function name,\ndocstring, or NL description, and must recover the rule purely from computational behavior."
        },
        {
          "shape": "33",
          "paragraph": 2,
          "text": "◦ Induction replaces code-pattern reuse.",
          "page": 2,
          "quote": "This requires abductive reasoning from the specific to the general, in which the composition and reuse strategies effective in NL2Code become inapplicable."
        },
        {
          "shape": "33",
          "paragraph": 3,
          "text": "◦ Generalization requires passing unseen tests.",
          "page": 4,
          "quote": "A candidate is considered a solution if and only if f (pˆ) = 1.0\non the visible set and it additionally passes all held-out tests—the latter serving as a generalization\ncheck against overfitting to spurious patterns in the visible examples."
        },
        {
          "shape": "33",
          "paragraph": 4,
          "text": "• DIO-Agent evolves programs through a curriculum.",
          "page": 2,
          "quote": "This evolutionary process is organized as a curriculum of increasing difficulty, with concrete error signals from execution\ngrounding each mutation in debugging evidence rather than scalar rewards."
        },
        {
          "shape": "33",
          "paragraph": 5,
          "text": "◦ Mutations explicitly discourage memorization.",
          "page": 5,
          "quote": "The LLM is\nalso explicitly instructed to avoid exact input matching, memorized lookup tables, and other hardcoded solutions tied to seen examples."
        },
        {
          "shape": "33",
          "paragraph": 6,
          "text": "◦ Simpler hypotheses precede complex constructs.",
          "page": 5,
          "quote": "This makes the mutation operator prefer the smallest hypothesis that explains the current evidence before moving to more complex control flow."
        },
        {
          "shape": "33",
          "paragraph": 7,
          "text": "• Benchmark gains span difficulty levels and LLMs.",
          "page": 10,
          "quote": "Extensive experiments show that DIO-Agent consistently outperforms existing program-by-example and\nevolution-agent baselines across all difficulty levels and various LLMs, while substantially surpassing test-time scaling strategies with equivalent sampling budgets."
        },
        {
          "shape": "33",
          "paragraph": 8,
          "text": "◦ Execution errors ground program refinement.",
          "page": 2,
          "quote": "This evolutionary process is organized as a curriculum of increasing difficulty, with concrete error signals from execution\ngrounding each mutation in debugging evidence rather than scalar rewards."
        },
        {
          "shape": "33",
          "paragraph": 9,
          "text": "• Uses: reverse engineering, legacy migration and scientific discovery.",
          "page": 1,
          "quote": "From migrating legacy systems with lost source code\nand reverse-engineering black-box APIs, to abducing algorithms from scientific observations and\ncapturing user intent through input-output demonstrations, these scenarios span system evolution,\nscientific discovery, and human-computer interaction (Collie et al., 2020)."
        }
      ],
      "protected": [
        "45"
      ],
